1.3.18.m:
Now then, we shall explain the division of the sacrificial animal for pressing: / having cut out the portions, the jaws along with the tongue belong to the Prastotṛ; / the throat along with the palate belongs to the Pratihartṛ; / the eagle-shaped breast belongs to the Udgātṛ; / the right flank with the shoulder belongs to the Adhvaryu; / the left to the assistant singers; / the left shoulder to the Pratiprasthātṛ; / the right buttock and the three third-ribs belong to the brahman priest; / the lower leg to the Brāhmaṇācchaṃsin; / the thigh to the Potṛ; / the left buttock to the Hotṛ; / the lower leg to the Maitrāvaruṇa; / the upper leg to the Acchāvāka; / the right foreleg to the Neṣṭṛ; / the left to the Sadasya; / the seat and the spine belong to the householder; / the tail-portion to the wife; / she has it received by a brahmin; / the entrails, the heart, the two kidneys, the fingers, and the right arm belong to the Āgnīdhra; / the left to the Ātreya; / the two right feet to the householder who bestows the vow; / the two left feet to the mistress of the house who bestows the vow; / theirs together is the lip;

the householder assigns it [to them].

The neck-vertebrae and the three ribs belong to the Grāvastut;

and three ribs and half of the rectum belong to the Unnetṛ.

What is beyond this belongs to the Camasādhvaryus; the lungs to the slaughterer;

the hide belongs to him who calls out the next day's pressing.

Thus indeed thirty-six are accomplished.

A cow has thirty-six cut portions;

the bṛhatī metre has thirty-six syllables.

Connected with the bṛhatī, indeed, is the heavenly world.

Through the bṛhatī, indeed, the gods sacrifice in the heavenly world;

through the bṛhatī they find firm standing in the heavenly world.

They find firm standing with offspring and cattle, who divide it thus.

Now, if otherwise than this a man of bad conduct, or wrong-doers, or eaters of leftovers, or other people should tear it apart, just so their sacrificial animal, being torn apart, becomes unheavenly.

Devabhāga Śruta-Ṛṣi indeed knew this division of the sacrificial animal.

1.4.7.m:
Through faith the gods fashioned the initiatory rite; from Aditi the introductory rite;
from Soma the purchase [of Soma];
from Viṣṇu the guest-offering;
from the Āditya the Pravargya;
from Svadhā the Upasads;
from Agni and Soma the day of preparation;
from the gods who come in the morning the morning recitation;
from the Vasus the morning pressing;
from the Rudras the midday pressing;
from the Ādityas the evening soma-pressing;
from Varuṇa the concluding bath;
from Aditi the concluding offering;
from Mitra and Varuṇa the barren cow to be bound [for sacrifice];
from Tvaṣṭṛ the offering to Tvaṣṭṛ;
from the Devīs and the Devikās the oblations to the deities;
from Desire the ten-day Atirātra;
from the heavenly world the rising offering.
This indeed is the birth of the Agniṣṭoma.
He who thus knows this birth of the Agniṣṭoma, having become of the same self and the same world with the Agniṣṭoma, goes to the gods, so says the Brāhmaṇa. || 7

1.4.9.m:
Those who undertake this attain union and the same world with the god, the heavenly world.

That indeed is the origin of the Agniṣṭoma.

He who knows this origin of the Agniṣṭoma thus, having obtained the Agniṣṭoma, firmly stands in the heavenly world.

He firmly stands with offspring and livestock who knows thus.

Having become of one self and one world with the Agniṣṭoma, he attains the gods. Thus the Brāhmaṇa. || 8

From day and night the gods fashioned the opening Atirātra;
from the half-months, the twenty-fourth day;
from the Brahman, the Abhiplava;
from the Kṣatra, the Pṛṣṭhya;
from Agni, the Abhijit;
from the waters, the Svarasāman days;
from the sun, the Viṣuvant day;
the reversed Svarasāman days have been explained;
from Indra, the Viśvajit;
the Pṛṣṭhya and Abhiplava have been explained;
from Mitra and Varuṇa, the Go and Āyus days;
from the All-gods, the ten-day period;
from the quarters, the six-day Pṛṣṭhya of the ten-day period;
from these worlds, the three-day Chandoma;
from the year, the tenth day;
from Prajāpati, the Mahāvrata;
from the heavenly world, the concluding Atirātra.
That indeed is the origin of the year.
He who knows this origin of the year thus, having become of one self and one world with the year, attains the gods. Thus the Brāhmaṇa. || 9

1.4.14.m:
Now, as to this: having undertaken the twenty-fourth day, if one should undertake the Mahāvrata without undertaking the viṣuvant, how does it not result in transgression?
He should say: By that very atirātra which they undertake prior to the viṣuvant.
From the abhiplava the pṛṣṭhya is fashioned;
from the pṛṣṭhya the abhijit;
from the abhijit the svarasāman [days];
from the svarasāman [days] the viṣuvant;
from the viṣuvant the svarasāman [days];
from the svarasāman [days] the viśvajit;
from the viśvajit the pṛṣṭhya and abhiplava;
from the pṛṣṭhya and abhiplava the go and āyus [days];
from the go and āyus [days] the ten-day period;
from the ten-day period the Mahāvrata;
from the Mahāvrata the concluding atirātra.
The concluding atirātra is for [attaining] the heavenly world, for proper food, for a firm standing.

1.4.15.m:
Now, as to this: when one undertakes the twenty-fourth day, and not undertaking the viṣuvant, undertakes the Mahāvrata, how does it not result in an incomplete vow?
One should say: 'By that very atirātra which they undertake before the viṣuvant.'
From the abhiplava the pṛṣṭhya is produced;
from the pṛṣṭhya, the abhijit;
from the abhijit, the svarasāmans;
from the svarasāmans, the viṣuvant;
from the viṣuvant, the svarasāmans;
from the svarasāmans, the viśvajit;
from the viśvajit, the pṛṣṭhya and abhiplava;
from the pṛṣṭhya and abhiplava, the go and āyus;
from the go and āyus, the ten-night rite.
Then the Mahāvrata did not stay for the gods, [saying]: 'How have you approached the viṣuvant with rising stomas, and me with reversed ones?'
Those gods were almost in despair here:
'Let us discover that sacrifice, that rising stoma, by which we may reach this day.'
Then they saw this twelve-night rite of rising stomas;
they took it;
they sacrificed with it.
Then it stood still for them.
The Mahāvrata stays for him,
he attains firm standing,
he attains firm standing with offspring and cattle, who knows thus.

1.4.18.m:
Now this is the great eagle.
Those six months which they undertake before the viṣuvant, that is its right wing;
and those six which they undertake turned back, later, that is its left wing.
The viṣuvant is the body of the year, the two wings are its limbs.
Where the body is, there are the two wings;
where the two wings are, there is the body;
the body does not exceed the two wings,
nor do the two wings exceed the body.
Just so, [if asked] whether it belongs to the earlier or later days, one should say: 'It belongs both to the earlier and to the later.'

1.4.21.m:
Now begins the mounting up of the days:
By the introductory atirātra they mount up to the concluding atirātra; by the twenty-fourth day, to the Mahāvrata;
by the abhiplava, to the higher abhiplava;
by the pṛṣṭhya, to the higher pṛṣṭhya;
by the abhijit, to the [other] abhijit; by the svarasāmans, to the subsequent svarasāmans.
Now, [if asked:] 'How may we reach this day: does the day of the viṣuvant belong to the earlier or to the later days?' One should say: 'It belongs both to the earlier and to the later.'
Such indeed is this year.

1.4.22.m:
Now begins the carrying forward of the days:
The introductory atirātra carries forward to the twenty-fourth day;
the twenty-fourth day, to the abhiplava;
the abhiplava, to the pṛṣṭhya;
the pṛṣṭhya, to the abhijit;
the abhijit, to the svarasāmans;
the svarasāmans, to the viṣuvant;
the viṣuvant, to the svarasāmans;
the svarasāmans, to the viśvajit;
the viśvajit, to the pṛṣṭhya and abhiplava;
the pṛṣṭhya and abhiplava, to the go and āyus;
the go and āyus, to the ten-night rite;
the ten-night rite, to the Mahāvrata;
the Mahāvrata, to the concluding atirātra;
the concluding atirātra [leads] to the heavenly world, to food, to firm standing.

1.4.23.m:
The Ādityas and the Aṅgirases were contending for the heavenly world: 'We shall go first to heaven! We first!'
Those Ādityas sailed over (abhyaplavanta) to the heavenly world by means of light sāmans, by four stomas, by two pṛṣṭhas.
Because they sailed over, therefore it is the abhiplava.
Close behind them, the Aṅgirases reached (abhyaspṛśanta) the heavenly world by means of heavy sāmans, by all the stomas, by all the pṛṣṭhas.
Because they reached it, therefore it is the spṛśya.
Indeed, being spṛśya, they cryptically call it pṛṣṭhya,
for the gods love the cryptic, as it were, and hate the direct.
From the abhiplava, the pṛṣṭhya was fashioned;
from the pṛṣṭhya, the abhijit;
from the abhijit, the svarasāmans;
from the svarasāmans, the viṣuvant;
from the viṣuvant, the svarasāmans;
from the svarasāmans, the viśvajit;
from the viśvajit, the pṛṣṭhya and abhiplava;
from the pṛṣṭhya and abhiplava, the go and āyus [days];
from the go and āyus, the ten-night period.
These, indeed, are the sacrificial wildernesses, the sacrificial chasms.
Between them there is an interval of a hundred chariot-lengths each. Just as those who have entered wildernesses, with hunger and thirst crushing their evil, drift about,
even so indeed do those drift about who undertake [the rite] without knowing.
Now as for those who undertake it knowing: just as they cross over from stream to stream, from dry land to dry land, from level ground to level ground, from comfort to comfort, from danger-free to danger-free, even so indeed do they [think]: 'May we reach the conclusion of the year!' Thus the Brāhmaṇa.

1.5.4.m:
His two hands are the concluding Atirātra sacrifice,

The year is indeed the Puruṣa. His out-breath is the introductory Atirātra sacrifice, for with the out-breath people set out. Speech is the introductory day: whatever one undertakes, speech undertakes; by speech alone one undertakes it. His right hand is the Abhiplava: his morning pressing is this [joint], his midday pressing is this, his evening pressing is this, in the seat of the gāyatrī; therefore this [finger] is shortest for him. His morning pressing is this, his midday pressing is this, his evening pressing is this, in the seat of the triṣṭubh; therefore this [finger] is tallest for him. His morning pressing is this, his midday pressing is this, his evening pressing is this, in the seat of the jagatī; therefore this [finger] is taller of the two. His morning pressing is this, his midday pressing is this, his evening pressing is this, in the seat of the paṅkti; broad, as it were, is the paṅkti; therefore this [finger] is the broadest of them. His morning pressing is this, his midday pressing is this, his evening pressing is this, in the seat of the virāj; food is prosperity;

for obtaining food-sustenance and prosperity;

therefore this [thumb] is the thickest of them.

His morning pressing is this, his midday pressing is this, his evening pressing is this,

The hyper-metre is indeed the foundation of metres;

therefore this palm is the broadest.

His morning pressing is this, his midday pressing is this, his evening pressing is this:

the Abhiplava is this one from here, this one from there; the Pṛṣṭhya is the self from here.

For it is with the limbs that one swims, as it were;

His right ear is the Abhijit.

The white part of his right eye is the first Svarasāman;

the pupil is the third.

The nostrils are the Viṣuvant; the pupil is the first reversed Svarasāman;

His left ear is the Viśvajit.

1.5.7.m:
Now next, the successions of sacrifices:
The fire-establishment;
from the fire-establishment, the full-spoon oblation;
from the full-spoon oblation, the agnihotra;
from the agnihotra, the New- and Full-Moon sacrifices;
from the New- and Full-Moon sacrifices, the first-fruits sacrifice;
from the first-fruits sacrifice, the four-monthly sacrifices;
from the four-monthly sacrifices, the animal sacrifice;
from the animal sacrifice, the agniṣṭoma;
from the agniṣṭoma, the royal consecration;
from the royal consecration, the drink-of-strength;
from the drink-of-strength, the horse sacrifice;
from the horse sacrifice, the human sacrifice;
from the human sacrifice, the all-sacrifice;
from the all-sacrifice, [sacrifices] with sacrificial fees;
from those with sacrificial fees, [sacrifices] without sacrificial fees;
those without sacrificial fees were established upon the one with a thousandfold sacrificial fee.
These indeed are the successions of sacrifices.
He who thus knows these successions of sacrifices, having become of the same self and dwelling in the same world with sacrifice, merges into the gods. Thus the Brāhmaṇa. || 7

1.5.12.m:
"You are the falcon, whose metre is the gāyatrī;
I touch you;
bring me safely across in well-being."
When he says, "You are the falcon," he indeed speaks of Soma.
He, having become Agni, causes [creatures] to lie down in this world.
Because he causes [them] to lie down, therefore he is the falcon (śyena);
that is the falcon-nature of the falcon.
When he says, "whose metre is the gāyatrī, I touch you," he touches [him] as being Agni in this world with the gāyatrī metre, with the god-Vasus, at the morning pressing.
When he says, "bring me safely across in well-being," [he means] with the gāyatrī metre, with the god-Vasus, at the morning pressing, in this world, with the god Agni, "bring me safely across in well-being"; thereby, with the gāyatrī metre, with the god-Vasus, at the morning pressing, in this world, with the god Agni, well-being falls to the lot of him who knows thus.

1.5.13.m:
Then, at the midday pavamāna laud, he makes [the sacrificer] recite: "You are the sovereign ruler, whose metre is the triṣṭubh;
I touch you;
bring me safely across in well-being."
When he says, "You are the sovereign ruler," he indeed speaks of Soma.
He, having become Vāyu, shines sovereignly in the intermediate space.
Because he shines sovereignly, therefore he is the sovereign ruler (samrāj);
that is the sovereign-ruler-nature of the sovereign ruler.
When he says, "whose metre is the triṣṭubh, I touch you," he touches [him] as being Vāyu in the intermediate space with the triṣṭubh metre, with the god-Rudras, at the midday pressing.
When he says, "bring me safely across in well-being," [he means] with the triṣṭubh metre, with the god-Rudras, at the midday pressing, in the intermediate space, with the god Vāyu, "bring me safely across in well-being"; thereby, with the triṣṭubh metre, with the god-Rudras, at the midday pressing, in the intermediate space, with the god Vāyu, well-being falls to the lot of him who knows thus. || 13

1.5.14.m:
Then, at the Ārbhava pavamāna laud, he makes [the sacrificer] recite: "You are the sunlit realm; you are the movable domain, whose metre is the jagatī;
I touch you;
bring me safely across in well-being."
When he says, "You are the sunlit realm (svara)," he indeed speaks of Soma.
He, having become the sun, sounds forth (svarati) in yonder world.
Because he sounds forth, therefore he is svara;
that is the svara-nature of svara.
When he says, "You are the movable domain (gaya)," he indeed speaks of Soma.
He, having become the moon, goes (gacchati) to all the worlds.
Because he goes, therefore he is gaya;
that is the gaya-nature of gaya.
When he says, "whose metre is the jagatī, I touch you," he touches [him] as being the sun in yonder world with the jagatī metre, with the god-Ādityas, at the evening soma-pressing.
When he says, "bring me safely across in well-being," [he means] with the jagatī metre, with the god-Ādityas, at the evening soma-pressing, in yonder world, with the god Sūrya, "bring me safely across in well-being"; thereby, with the jagatī metre, with the god-Ādityas, at the evening soma-pressing, in yonder world, with the god Sūrya, well-being falls to the lot of him who knows thus. || 14

2.1.1.m:
Thus ends the fifth prapāṭhaka in the First Part of the Gopatha Brāhmaṇa belonging to the Atharvaveda.

Now, when he casts away a blade of grass from the seat of the brahman priest, he thereby purifies it.
Then he sits down, saying: 'Here I sit down in the seat of Arvāgvasu.'
Arvāgvasu was indeed the brahman priest of the gods, Parāgvasu of the asuras.
Him he thus seats first, saying:
'Perform an unharmed sacrifice.'
Then, having sat down, he murmurs: 'Bṛhaspati is the brahman priest.'
Bṛhaspati Āṅgirasa is indeed the brahman priest of the gods.
In him he thus seeks permission. While the praṇītā water is being carried forward, he restrains his speech until the call of the maker of the oblation.
This is indeed the door of the sacrifice.
He thereby makes it not empty.
And when the Sviṣṭakṛt has been sacrificed, until the prompting of the after-offerings.
This is indeed the second door of the sacrifice.
He thereby makes it not empty.
That the enclosing-sticks are laid around is for the protection of the sacrifice.
He lays around the enclosing-sticks for the self-sufficiency of the sacrifice.
He wipes the enclosing-sticks;
he thereby purifies them:
thrice the middle one;
three are these vital breaths;
he thereby wins the vital breaths;
thrice the southern one;
three are the worlds;
he thereby wins the worlds;

he thereby wins the worlds of the gods;

thrice he fans [the fire]; three are the paths of the gods;

They indeed become twelve.

Twelve months are the year.

2.1.9.m:
One for whom, when the oblation is set apart, the moon rises in the east, should divide those rice-grains into three parts.
Those that are middle-sized, he should offer on eight potsherds to Agni the Giver;
those that are largest, in curd as a caru to Indra the Bestower;
those that are smallest, in boiled milk as a caru to Viṣṇu Śipiviṣṭa.
These [grains] are cattle left over.
He obtains them;
he secures them.
Agni is the giver of the middle one, Indra the bestower of the greatest.
That which is small among cattle, that belongs to Viṣṇu Śipiviṣṭa.
That he obtains;
he secures cattle.

2.1.18.m:
For she is the image of the year.

For this [heifer] is semen not yet brought forth.

Now then, this Apratiratha hymn [beginning]: 'Indra’s arms are stout, virile…'
By this indeed Indra conquered the asuras.
He becomes unopposed; by this the sacrificer conquers his rival.
He should sacrifice it in battle; he becomes unopposed.
By this indeed Bharadvāja girded Pratardana;
he became a ruler of a realm.
Whomever he might wish: 'May he be a ruler of a realm,' him he should gird with this;
he becomes a ruler of a realm.
By this indeed Indra conquered the virāj metre.
He recites only ten [verses];
ten-syllabled is the virāj; by this the sacrificer deprives his rival of virāj-power.
And as to that, some recite eleven [verses];
eleven-syllabled indeed is the triṣṭubh; the thunderbolt has the nature of the triṣṭubh;
with the thunderbolt indeed he wards off the demons.
From the south indeed the demons sought to strike the gods' sacrifice;
them they struck away with the Apratiratha hymn.
Therefore the brahman priest walks muttering the Apratiratha hymn.
When the brahman priest walks muttering the Apratiratha hymn, it is for the winning of the sacrifice, for the smiting of the demons, for the smiting of the demons.

2.1.24.m:
Now, that he gives a bull [as sacrificial fee]: this sacrificial rite belongs indeed to Indra.

Now, as to their performing the Pitṛyajña in the afternoon: the ancestors share in the afternoon;
therefore they perform the Pitṛyajña in the afternoon.
Regarding this they say: 'Since the ancestors share in the dark fortnight, why do they sacrifice to them in the bright fortnight?'
These ancestors are gods;
therefore they sacrifice to them in the bright fortnight.
Now, as to his reciting one kindling verse three times: the ancestors are once only;
therefore he recites one kindling verse three times.
Now, as to his not reciting the ancestral lineage of the sacrificer: [it is] lest he exclude the sacrificer.
Now, as to his invoking Soma accompanied by the ancestors, or the ancestors accompanied by Soma, the Barhiṣad ancestors, and the Agniṣvātta ancestors:
some do not invoke 'his own greatness', saying, 'This is the greatness of the sacrificer.' But the settled rule is that one should invoke it,
for this is the greatness of Agni.
He causes [the Adhvaryu] to announce with 'Oṃ svadhā!'
He responds with 'Astu svadhā!'
For the exclamation svadhā belongs to the ancestors.
Now, as to his removing the two [offerings] accompanied by sacrificial grass from the fore-offerings and after-offerings: sacrificial grass is offspring; [it is done] lest he place offspring among the ancestors.
They amount to six;
six indeed are the seasons;
the seasons are the ancestors;
[it is] for the attainment of the ancestors. || 24

2.1.25.m:
Now, that the two portions of clarified butter are accompanied by the 'living' [verses]: thereby he makes the sacrificer himself live.
Now, that for each oblation there are three offering-verses: with the first he invites them;
with the second he makes them go;
with the third he sends them forth.
Moreover, thereby he separates the sacrifice to the gods from the sacrifice to the ancestors.
Moreover, the sacrifice to the ancestors indeed ends towards the south;
thereby they make it end towards the north.
Now, that at the end he offers to Agni Kavyavāhana: this Sviṣṭakṛt belongs to the ancestors;
therefore he offers to Agni Kavyavāhana at the end.
Now, that having invoked the Iḍā and smelled it, they do not eat of it: cattle are the Iḍā;
[they do so thinking:] 'Lest I exclude cattle.'
Now, that in the Sūktavāka he recites the blessings of the sacrificer: [he does so thinking:] 'Lest I exclude the sacrificer.'
Now, that they do not perform the joint offerings with the wife: [they do so thinking:] 'Lest I exclude the wife.'
Now, that they cleanse themselves with a purifier: water is pacification and medicine;
it is pacification and medicine that is performed at the end in the sacrifice.
Now, that the Adhvaryu pours out [water] for the ancestors: the living ancestors indeed follow; the human ancestors flow along [with it].
Moreover, thereby they separate this sacrifice to the gods from the sacrifice to the ancestors.
Moreover, the sacrifice to the ancestors indeed ends towards the south;
thereby they make it end towards the north.
Now, that having stepped forward towards the east they attend upon Āditya: the world of the gods is Āditya;
the ancestors are the world of the ancestors;
thereby they cross over to the world of the gods from the world of the ancestors.
Now, that having stepped forward towards the south they attend upon the fires: it is out of affection that they go to the realm of the gods at the end.

Now, that having stepped forward towards the north they sacrifice with the Traiambaka offerings: thereby they gratify Rudra in his own direction.

Moreover, thereby they separate this sacrifice to the gods from the sacrifice to the ancestors.

Moreover, the sacrifice to the ancestors indeed ends towards the south;

thereby they make it end towards the north.

Now, that at the end he sacrifices with an iṣṭi sacrifice to Aditi: Aditi is this [earth].

2.1.26.m:
Upon this [earth] he establishes him firmly at the end.

Now, that thereafter he sacrifices with the Full-Moon sacrifice: thus his sacrifice with the Sākamedhas is performed in the waxing fortnight.

It is this thirteenth month that he attains when he sacrifices with the Śunāsīrīya.
As large as this thirteenth month is, so large is the year.
Now, that they lead forward the fire: that very fire which they churn at the Vaiśvadeva, that same one they lead forward.
Regarding the fact that it is churned, its Brāhmaṇa has been explained.
If, however, it is not churned, the Full-Moon sacrifice becomes the procedure.
The Full-Moon sacrifice is indeed a firm standing;
[it is] for firm standing indeed. Now, that he sacrifices to Vāyu: breath is Vāyu;
by that he gratifies breath.
Now, that he sacrifices to Śunāsīra: Śunāsīra is the year;
by that he gratifies the year.
Now, that he sacrifices to Sūrya: Sūrya is that one who burns yonder;
by that he gratifies him.
Now, that he gives a white [cow as] sacrificial fee: that very form of this [sun] is produced.
Now, that they provide a substitute for expiation: they thereby make a means of well-being;
it is the pacification of the sacrifice for the healing of the sacrificer.
By these very Cāturmāsya sacrifices the gods attained all desires, all iṣṭi sacrifices, all immortality.
This twenty-four-fold year is Prajāpati, which is the Cāturmāsya sacrifices.
His mouth is the Vaiśvadeva;
his two arms are the Varuṇapraghāsas;
these three iṣṭi sacrifices are the out-breath, the in-breath, and the diffused breath;
his body is the Mahāhavis;
his firm standing is the Śunāsīrīya.
This very Prajāpati is the year, which is the Cāturmāsya sacrifices.

Thereby, through the all he attains the all, who knows thus, and who, knowing thus, sacrifices with the Cāturmāsya sacrifices, sacrifices with the Cāturmāsya sacrifices, sacrifices with the Cāturmāsya sacrifices.

2.2.1.m:
Thus ends the first prapāṭhaka in the second part of the Gopatha Brāhmaṇa belonging to the Atharvaveda.

Oṃ. The fires of the fire-establisher crave meat;
they brood first over this sacrificer.
He who seizes this animal for Indra and Agni every sixth month, by that he redeems himself from being swallowed by Indra and Agni.
One who desires long life should seize it;
Indra and Agni are out-breath and in-breath;
he places out-breath and in-breath in himself;
he becomes long-lived.
One who desires offspring should seize it;
Indra and Agni are out-breath and in-breath;
offspring are born following out-breath and in-breath;
he becomes possessed of offspring.
One who desires cattle should seize it;
Indra and Agni are out-breath and in-breath;
cattle are born following out-breath and in-breath;
he becomes possessed of cattle.
One who desires, 'May I be free from illness in the world of the ancestors,' should seize a parrot-green or castrated [animal] sacred to Yama.
By this indeed Yama prospered in yonder world;
he prospers in the world of the ancestors.
One who desires offspring should seize a mare sacred to Tvaṣṭṛ.
Prajāpati, when desiring to create offspring, did not find a second mate;
he saw a mare sacred to Tvaṣṭṛ;
for Tvaṣṭṛ is the generator of forms;
with it he created offspring;

Possessed of offspring, possessed of a mate he becomes who knows thus and who, knowing thus, seizes this [animal].

He seizes optional animals away from their origin, who seizes an optional animal without having sacrificed with [the victim] for Indra and Agni; seizing after having sacrificed leads to success.

2.2.3.m:
Five times he cuts portions;
fivefold is the sacrifice;
for fivefold they apportioned them.
'To Āpati I take thee,' he says;
Āpati is breath;
thereby he gratifies breath.
'To Paripati thee,' he says;
Paripati is mind;
thereby he gratifies mind.
'To Tanūnapāt,' he says;
for they apportioned those bodies of theirs.
'To the mighty one,' he says;
for they apportioned [them] for capacity.
'To the most vigorous of capacity,' he says;
for they apportioned that most vigorous [part] of their self.
'Unassailable,' he says;
for this is unassailable.
'Not to be assailed,' he says;
for this is not to be assailed.
'The vigor of the gods,' he says;
for this is the vigor of the gods.
'Protector against curse,' he says;
for this is a protector against curse.

'Free from blame,' he says.

"Beyond blame," he says, for it is indeed beyond blame.

"May the Lord of Consecration approve my consecration; may the Lord of Ascetic Heat approve my ascetic heat; directly may I attain the truth; place me in welfare," he says; this is precisely according to the Yajus formula.

2.2.4.m:
The gods, having made clarified butter into a thunderbolt, struck Soma.
The two ladles are the two arms.
Therefore the two ladles did not partake of the offering of Soma.
Soma would be slain;
therefore he brought the two ladles and the clarified butter near.
Near indeed, as it were, they perform this for him when they perform with the Tānūnaptra.
"May stalk after stalk swell for thee, divine Soma, for Indra who finds unique wealth," he says;
whatever of him dwindles away, whatever is measured out, just that of him they swell by this.
"May Indra swell for thee, mayest thou swell for Indra," he says;
they swell both Indra and Soma.
"Swell us, thy friends, with gain, with wisdom, with offspring, with wealth," he says;
his friends are indeed the priests;
them indeed they swell for him by this.
"Farewell, divine Soma! May I attain the conclusion of the pressing!" he says;
he thereby utters this blessing.
They indeed slip away from this world who swell Soma;
for swollen Soma has the intermediate space as his deity.
"Sought are riches, sought are precious things, forward to the impulse, to Bhaga! / Truth to the speakers of truth! Homage to heaven, homage to earth!"—
having paid homage to Heaven and Earth, he firmly stands in this world, he firmly stands.
